        "caught": 2,
        "runs": 2,
        "valid": 2,
        "methods": [
          "Keep NIR as the main method for the existing pilot, since it is already installed and fast. Build a designed set of blended powders covering the new API and blend across the target concentration range, plus the old formulation as a control. Quantify the failure of the old model on the new blend, then test transfer approaches (bias or slope correction, or augmented PLS with the new samples). Evaluate Raman on the same samples as a complementary method if the new API has strong, selective Raman bands.",
          "Primary: NIR (existing) with a calibration-transfer assessment on the new blend. Secondary: Raman evaluated for its own calibration on the new blend, and optionally as a combined NIR+Raman model if both are feasible in-line."
        ]
      },
      "H02": {
        "trap": "Clear whey and opaque concentrate need different interfaces (transmission/transflection vs reflection)",
        "caught": 2,
        "runs": 2,
        "valid": 2,
        "methods": [
          "NIR spectroscopy (about 900-2500 nm, with the water combination band near 1940 nm and the protein and fat CH/NH bands near 1700-1750 nm and 2300-2350 nm), with separate calibrations per stream. Transmission for whey, diffuse reflectance or transflectance for concentrate. Spectra should be taken at a controlled product temperature.",
          "Fibre-coupled NIR (roughly 900-2500 nm) as the main method for protein and fat in both streams. For the whey, NIR transmission or transflectance with a fixed short path, and optionally UV-Vis at about 280 nm for protein. For the concentrate, NIR diffuse reflectance or transflectance with a very short gap, since transmission saturates in opaque high-scatter samples."
        ]
      },
